vote":"aye"},{"name":"Simon Birmingham","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10044","person_slug":"simon-birmingham","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Slade Brockman","party":"DPRES","person_id":"tvfy_10900","person_slug":"slade-brockman","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Stirling Griff","party":"Centre Alliance","person_id":"tvfy_10851","person_slug":"stirling-griff","vote":"no"},{"name":"Wendy Askew","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10914","person_slug":"wendy-askew","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Zed Seselja","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10822","person_slug":"zed-seselja","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Business - Senate Standing Orders - Make temporary orders permanent","noes":9,"question":"The majority voted in favour of a [motion](https://www.openaustralia.org.au/senate/?id=2021-12-02.84.2) below, which means it passed. [Standing orders](https://peo.gov.au/understand-our-parliament/how-parliament-works/parliament-at-work/standing-orders/) are the usual procedural rules of parliament. ### Motion text > *That the amendments to standing orders relating to the routine of business, formal motions and notices of motion agreed to as a temporary order on 24 June 2021 take effect as a permanent order, with the following changes:* > > *(1) Standing order 66(5)(a), after \"legislation\", insert \"by the Senate\".* > > *(2) Standing order 66(5)(c), after \"the conduct of\", insert \"Senate or joint\".* > > *(3) Omit standing order 76(7A), substitute:* > >> *(7A) A general business notice of motion shall not exceed 200 words unless it is a motion for which a request for formality may be made in accordance with standing order 66(5).*","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-7571","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2021-12-02/4"}
